**4. `Command.cpp` `CmdPartRuledSurface` — `curve2` assigned twice, `curve1` never assigned.**

```cpp
if (!subnames1.empty()) {
    curve2 = Part::Feature::getTopoShape(docobj1, subnames1[0], ...).getShape();  // <-- curve2, should be curve1
    link1 = subnames1[0];
}
if (subnames1.size() == 2) {
    curve2 = ... subnames1[1] ...;  // curve2 again
    link2 = subnames1[1];
}
```
Original code: first one was `curve1 = ... subnames1[0]`, second `curve2 = ... subnames1[1]`. The new code changed the first to `curve2` — so `curve1` is never assigned when subnames1 has elements, and when there are two subnames, curve2 gets overwritten with the second. This is a real regression: the first curve is lost. Concrete failure: creating a Ruled Surface with two sketches/edges — the first curve (curve1) is never set (stays default/uninitialized), leading to wrong geometry or the ruled surface using the wrong curve. Line in Command.cpp hunk `@@ -1972,11 +1971,24 @@`.

Let me double check the original: `curve1 = ... subnames1[0]` and `curve2 = ... subnames1[1]`. New: `curve2 = ... subnames1[0]` then `curve2 = ...subnames1[1]`. Yes — first assignment changed from curve1 to curve2. Defect.

### 4. `src/Mod/Part/Gui/TaskSweep.cpp` — loop body indexes `subnames[0]` for every iteration (visible in hunk)
Hunk `@@ -298,7 +298,13 @@` (line ~302). The for-loop iterates `it` over `subnames` but each `push_back` uses `subnames[0].c_str()`:
```cpp
for (std::vector<std::string>::const_iterator it = subnames.begin(); it != subnames.end(); ++it) {
    subShapes.push_back(Part::Feature::getTopoShape(docobj, subnames[0].c_str(), ...));
}
```
Note: this mis-indexing predates the diff (the old line also used `subnames[0]`), so it is not introduced here, but the changed hunk carries it forward: multi-profile sweeps push the same first profile repeatedly, yielding duplicate/incomplete sections. I flag it because it is a genuine defect present in the diff body, though pre-existing.

### 4. **src/Mod/PartDesign/App/FeatureSketchBased.cpp:711** — Calls `getShape` (forces `NoElementMap`) instead of `getTopoShape`

**Failure scenario**: In `getUpToShapeFromLinkSubList`, a sub-shape is retrieved by name via `getShape`, which forces `NoElementMap` flag. This resets the element map (`shape.resetElementMap(); shape.Tag = 0;`). The subsequent `face.makeElementFace()` relies on element tags to find the correct face — fails or returns wrong face.

```cpp
// Old: getTopoShape (noElementMap=false)
auto shape = Part::Feature::getTopoShape(ref, subString.c_str(), true);
// New: getShape (forces NoElementMap=true via getShape implementation)
auto shape = Part::Feature::getShape(ref, subString.c_str(), ...);
TopoShape face = shape;
face = face.makeElementFace();  // element map cleared → fails
```

